---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research Methodology & Hierarchy of Authority | Cover & Capital India"
  description="How research is conducted at Cover & Capital India. Our 4-tier hierarchy of authority, primary legislative documentation, and cash-flow verification standards."
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 sm:py-16">
    <div class="space-y-8">
      <div class="border-b border-[#E5E3D8] pb-6">
        <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#0D5C46]">Research Framework</span>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold font-serif text-[#0F172A] mt-2">
          Research Methodology & Hierarchy of Authority
        </h1>
        <p class="text-base text-[#475569] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          How Cover & Capital India verifies insurance contracts, statutory tax amendments, and financial mathematics.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="editorial-prose space-y-6">
        <h2>The 4-Tier Hierarchy of Authority</h2>
        <p>
          We do not treat search-engine summaries or AI memory as authoritative proof of current Indian law or insurance contracts. All writing adheres to this strict hierarchy:
        </p>

        <h3>Tier 1: Primary Legislation & Central Government Authority</h3>
        <p>
          Binding statutes passed by the Parliament of India, official Gazette of India notifications, and Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT) statutory circulars.
        </p>

        <h3>Tier 2: Statutory Financial Regulators</h3>
        <p>
          The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI), the Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI), and the Reserve Bank of India (RBI). We inspect official master circulars, public disclosure tables (Form L-1 to L-40), and Insurance Ombudsman rulings.
        </p>

        <h3>Tier 3: Verified Product Contracts</h3>
        <p>
          Official policy wordings and prospectuses filed by insurers under their IRDAI Unique Identification Number (UIN), rather than marketing one-pagers or advertising brochures.
        </p>

        <h3>Tier 4: Secondary Commentary</h3>
        <p>
          Academic finance research, actuarial papers from the Institute of Actuaries of India (IAI), and reputable investigative financial journalism. Secondary sources are used only for historical context or discovering questions that Tier 1 and 2 sources must answer.
        </p>

        <h2>Contractual vs Category Claims</h2>
        <p>
          We never universalize one insurer's policy terms across the entire industry. If an exclusion (e.g. hazardous adventure sports or specific critical illness definitions) applies to a specific insurer's policy bond, we cite that insurer's UIN explicitly rather than claiming all Indian policies share that clause.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
